# n2i-sec-audit skill config.
# Copy this file to <target repo root>/.n2i-sec-audit/config, next to the .git directory.
# The skill reads it when present. The first run offers to create it for you.
# The .n2i-sec-audit/ folder holds local, per-developer state. Add this line to the
# target repo's .gitignore:
#   .n2i-sec-audit/
# The folder can also hold a hand-written notes.md (personal repo gotchas), the progress.md
# step ledger, and last-scan.txt from the scanner.
# Keep secrets out of this folder. The skill warns if notes.md looks like it holds one.
# Every key is optional and falls back to the default shown. Format: KEY=value, one per line.

# Where the skill writes reports and where a re-audit looks for the previous one.
# REPORT_DIR=docs
# Glob, inside REPORT_DIR, that matches earlier reports.
# REPORT_GLOB=*security-review*.md

# Shared, committed file that records accepted risks (item ID, owner, date, review-by).
# ACCEPTED_RISKS=docs/security-accepted-risks.md

# Stacks to audit, space-separated (for example "dotnet angular" or "node").
# Default: auto-detect.
# STACKS=auto

# Directories to audit, relative to the repo root. Default: the whole repo.
# SCOPE_DIRS=backend frontend

# Globs the scanner and searches should skip, such as vendored or legacy code. The skill
# passes each one to scan.py as --exclude, and the report lists them. For permanent rules
# that the whole team shares, commit a .secauditignore file at the repo root instead.
# EXCLUDE_DIRS=old/* third_party/*

# Checklist IDs to skip, separated by spaces. Record the reason in notes.md.
# CHECK_SKIP=D8 D9

# Local or owned URL for the header and cookie check (one GET). A remote target also
# needs --allow-remote on the command line as proof you own it.
# SCAN_URL=http://localhost:5000

# Dependency severity that counts as FAIL in scan.py: critical | high | none.
# FAIL_ON=critical

# What to offer after the findings: ask | none. Default: ask (plan, tickets, hand-off, or stop).
# NEXT_STEP=ask
